#!/usr/local/bin/cbsd
#v0.5.8
# seance -- CBSD verb wrapper (decision D-2).
#
# This is the only file in the project that runs under cbsdsh. It does no
# seance work: it copies the facts CBSD knows about this node into the
# SEANCE_CBSD_* environment and execs the plain /bin/sh dispatcher, which is
# what every test tier and the pseudo-cluster drive directly.
#
# shellcheck shell=sh
# shellcheck disable=SC1090,SC1091,SC2034,SC2154
#   SC1090/SC1091: ${subrdir} and ${tools} are cbsdsh runtime paths supplied by
#     /usr/local/cbsd/cbsd.conf:39,43. There is no constant path to follow and
#     nothing for shellcheck to read; the sourced files are CBSD's, not ours.
#   SC2034: CBSDMODULE, CIXARG, CIXOPTARG, MYDESC and ADDHELP are read by
#     cbsdsh's own cixinit, not by this script. They are the verb's registration
#     header (modules/pkg.d/pkg:3-7), and unused-looking is what they look like.
#   SC2154: workdir, nodename, jailsysdir, jaildatadir, dbdir, etcdir,
#     CIX_DISTDIR, myversion, mode, CIX_OTHER_ARGS and *_CMD are supplied by the
#     cbsdsh environment (cbsd.conf:21-99) -- reading them is this file's whole
#     purpose. Nothing here assigns them, and nothing here should.
CBSDMODULE="seance"
CIXARG=""
CIXOPTARG="mode"
MYDESC="Guest succession: replication, death detection, fencing, promotion"
ADDHELP="
${H3_COLOR}Description${N0_COLOR}:

 seance gives a CBSD cluster crash-consistent guest succession. This node's
 CBSD facts are passed to the dispatcher in the environment; every verb prints
 a verdict line and exits 0 (ok), 1 (operation failed) or 2 (usage error).
 'promote' adds one more: 75, another promotion of that node already owns
 this host's lock.

${H3_COLOR}Options${N0_COLOR}:

 ${N2_COLOR}mode=${N0_COLOR} - verb to run. The verb may also be given bare. Verbs:
   repl    - one replication tick: snapshot the guests, finish any
             interrupted receive, send the increment to each heir, prune
             both ends, record the lag. This is the crontab target;
             '--now' ignores the per-guest cadence, '--guest' and '--peer'
             narrow it, '--dry-run' says what it would send and sends
             nothing;
   status  - the one screen: every guest, where it runs, how fresh its
             replica is on each heir, and whether the mesh still agrees
             about the configuration. '--tsv' for the machine-readable
             form. The exit code is the fleet's health;
   verify  - render the configuration seance expects and diff it against
             reality: the mesh matrix, the clock delta, one config file
             everywhere, the standby parents, the crontab line, the CARP
             vhids this node should carry and the devd(8) rules that act
             on them. 'verify --render cron|carp|devd' prints each of the
             three. verify NEVER writes -- not the crontab, not rc.conf,
             not devd's rules, not a ZFS property;
   promote - walk the succession ladder for a dead node's estate. Seven
             rungs, each logged and each able to stop it: debounce,
             quorum, the death probes, fencing, lineage, promotion,
             post. '--guest' narrows it to one guest.
             '--force[=<rung>,...]' overrides only the rungs it names, and
             may name only quorum, fence, lineage and kernel -- a host
             that answers the death probes is never fenced by force, and
             a fence driver that REFUSED is never overridden.
             '--auto' is the devd(8) path: a rung 0 checks that the fleet
             key 'auto' is 1 AND that this node's own auto_promote names
             the corpse, rung 1 waits and then re-reads CARP, and rungs
             1-4 must all be green. '--force' may NOT be combined with
             it -- a force is a human accepting a rung that could not
             answer, and there is no human on that path.
             ONE LADDER PER DEAD NODE PER HOST: the promotion runs under
             lockf(1), so a second one -- another operator, or devd's own
             automatic run -- exits 75 and names what is holding it,
             '--force' included;
   promote-event
           - INTERNAL: what a devd(8) CARP rule runs, once per MASTER
             transition, as 'promote-event <vhid>@<ifname>'. It maps the
             vhid to a node and either detaches 'promote <node> --auto'
             with daemon(8), logging to syslog under the tag seance, or
             notifies and does nothing. Bounded on every path, because
             devd(8) waits for its actions;
   failback- bring a guest home. Run it on the guest's home node once that
             node is back, gated, and not running the guest. The reverse
             stream lands in the live dataset, so seance measures what
             would be rolled back first and refuses unless
             '--discard-origin-writes' says otherwise;
   failback-assist
           - INTERNAL: the interim host's half of a failback, invoked over
             the mesh. 'stop', 'start', 'snapshot', 'unregister', 'release';
             'start' is how a refused failback puts the guest back up;
   gate    - the resurrection gate. For every guest whose home is this
             node, ask each LIVING peer whether it already claims that
             guest; withhold any that is claimed, and the whole estate if
             not one peer answers. '--check' says what it would do;
             '--release <guest>' releases one, if nobody still claims it;
   placement
           - which guests this node hosts away from home. '--remote'
             gathers the same from every living peer. This is the verb
             every peer answers over ssh, so it must be runnable as the
             ssh user on every node;
   config  - print the effective configuration and its verdict;
             'config --check' validates and lists every problem;
   setup   - an interactive (bsddialog) or headless editor for the config
             file. It only ever writes the target file -- never the system
             -- and runs 'config --check' on the result before writing;
             '--non-interactive' with '--set key=value' (repeatable) or
             '--answers <file>' is every screen's non-interactive twin;
             '--from <file>' seeds the walkthrough from an existing file;
             '--dump-answers <file>' records what was answered so it can be
             replayed with '--answers';
   version - print the seance version;
   help    - print dispatcher usage;

 Both spellings carry the arguments that follow the verb: 'mode=config --check'
 does the same thing as the bare 'config --check'. Bare is shorter; the mode=
 form is the framework's own idiom. The Examples below show each in full.

 The configuration file is \${SEANCE_CONF} if set, otherwise
 \${workdir}/etc/seance.conf; 'config --file <path>' overrides both.
 State lives in \${workdir}/var/db/seance and locks in
 \${workdir}/var/run/seance, both overridable with \${SEANCE_STATE_DIR}
 and \${SEANCE_RUN_DIR}.

${H3_COLOR}Examples${N0_COLOR}:

 # cbsd seance version
 # cbsd seance mode=version
 # cbsd seance status
 # cbsd seance repl --now
 # cbsd seance repl --guest web01 --dry-run
 # cbsd seance verify
 # cbsd seance verify --render cron
 # cbsd seance verify --render carp
 # cbsd seance verify --render devd
 # cbsd seance promote alpha
 # cbsd seance promote alpha --guest web01 --force=fence
 # cbsd seance promote-event 1@vtnet0
 # cbsd seance failback web01
 # cbsd seance gate --check
 # cbsd seance placement --remote
 # cbsd seance config
 # cbsd seance config --check
 # cbsd seance config --file /path/to/seance.conf
 # cbsd seance setup
 # cbsd seance setup --non-interactive --set cadence=600 --out /tmp/seance.conf
 # cbsd seance setup --non-interactive --from /path/to/seance.conf --allow-invalid
 # cbsd seance help

"

. "${subrdir}/nc.subr"
. "${tools}"
cixinit

# This file is reached through ${workdir}/modules/seance, a symlink planted by
# 'cbsd initenv' stage 8 (sudoexec/initenv:1386-1396), so $0 is the symlink and
# its directory is not the module directory. Resolve it -- the idiom is
# modules/zfsinstall.d/zfsinstall:28.
_MYSELF=$( ${REALPATH_CMD} "$0" )
_MYDIR=${_MYSELF%/*}

# CBSD facts, from /usr/local/cbsd/cbsd.conf: workdir (line 28-36),
# jailsysdir (71), jaildatadir (65), jailrcconfdir (67), dbdir (92), etcdir
# (73), CIX_DISTDIR (24), myversion (21); nodename is read by CBSD from
# ${workdir}/nodename (52).
SEANCE_CBSD_WORKDIR="${workdir}"
SEANCE_CBSD_NODENAME="${nodename}"
SEANCE_CBSD_JAILSYSDIR="${jailsysdir}"
SEANCE_CBSD_JAILDATADIR="${jaildatadir}"
SEANCE_CBSD_JAILRCCONFDIR="${jailrcconfdir}"
SEANCE_CBSD_DBDIR="${dbdir}"
SEANCE_CBSD_ETCDIR="${etcdir}"
SEANCE_CBSD_DISTDIR="${CIX_DISTDIR}"
SEANCE_CBSD_VERSION="${myversion}"
export SEANCE_CBSD_WORKDIR SEANCE_CBSD_NODENAME SEANCE_CBSD_JAILSYSDIR
export SEANCE_CBSD_JAILDATADIR SEANCE_CBSD_JAILRCCONFDIR
export SEANCE_CBSD_DBDIR SEANCE_CBSD_ETCDIR
export SEANCE_CBSD_DISTDIR SEANCE_CBSD_VERSION

# 'cbsd seance mode=version' and 'cbsd seance version' must both work: CBSD has
# no two-level dispatch (PATH only, cbsd.conf:122), so a bare verb arrives in
# CIX_OTHER_ARGS.
#
# BOTH are passed on, in that order, and that is the whole of the fix this line
# once needed. CBSD turns every 'key=value' word named in CIXARG/CIXOPTARG into
# a shell variable and drops everything else into CIX_OTHER_ARGS
# (tools/getinfo:13, tools/task:53-60), so the two spellings arrive as:
#
#     cbsd seance config --check       mode=''        CIX_OTHER_ARGS='config --check'
#     cbsd seance mode=config --check  mode='config'  CIX_OTHER_ARGS='--check'
#
# Reading ${mode} alone answered the second by running 'config' without
# '--check': an operator asked for a validation, got a dump and exit 0, and
# nothing anywhere said the flag had gone nowhere. A verb that silently drops
# an argument is worse than one that refuses it. Pinned by
# tests/tier1/t_verb_wrapper.sh, which drives this file with a faked cbsdsh
# environment, and observed against real CBSD in docs/cbsd-module-notes.md §2.
#
# shellcheck disable=SC2086
#   Deliberate word splitting: ${mode} is one word and ${CIX_OTHER_ARGS} is the
#   whole bare argument list, which must reach the dispatcher as separate
#   arguments rather than as one.
exec ${_MYDIR}/bin/seance ${mode} ${CIX_OTHER_ARGS}
